dropped the body
--
N5008 [temp.names]/3.4: a `<' after a name is the start of a
template-argument-list if the name is "a terminal name ... in a type-only
context other than a nested-name-specifier"; [temp.res.general]/4.1: the
terminal name of a typename-specifier is in a type-only context.  So in
`typename traits<A>::rebind<int>', `rebind' names a member template
without the `template' keyword (P1787, applied to C++20; GCC accepts it
in every language mode, as here with --cpp11).  The front end reported
"found no match for symbol 'rebind'" and rejected the unit: the parser
consumed `typename' before reading the name, so the name was not known
to be in a type-only context, and `<int>' became a separate declarator.
Reference compilers: g++ 13 accepts it, and the program fails only its
WRONG assertion.  clang++ 18 and the clang 23 of the cxx23-gate image
reject it ("use 'template' keyword to treat 'rebind' as a dependent
template name"): Clang has not implemented this part of P1787.  Found
while reducing cpp11_map_of_move_only_mapped_type: cvise removed
libstdc++'s `template' from `::template rebind_alloc<'.  Fails on the round-163
build bin52.
